<!-- INTRO -->
<section class="intro-block" id="intro">
  <h2>Before You Begin</h2>
  <p>This course assumes you have never touched a violin. Every single instruction is written as if you are standing in a room with the instrument for the very first time. Nothing is skipped, nothing is assumed.</p>
  <p>Each week gives you a complete daily practice routine, broken into timed blocks. Each block is further expanded into step-by-step instructions — what to physically do, what to listen for, and what mistakes to avoid. You can click any practice block to reveal its full detail.</p>

  <div class="important-box">
    <strong>The Single Most Important Rule</strong>
    <p>Never practise through pain. A mild ache in unused muscles is normal. Sharp pain in your shoulder, wrist, elbow, or fingers is your body telling you to stop and re-examine your posture. Tension is the enemy of both good tone and injury prevention. If something hurts, stop, shake out your arms, and start again slowly with less force.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The structure of each day's practice follows a simple logic: <strong>warm up the body → build new technique → apply it to music → review and consolidate.</strong> This mirrors how professional musicians practise throughout their entire lives. You are not learning a shortcut version — you are learning the real thing, at a beginner's pace.</p>

  <div class="important-box">
    <strong>How to Use the Expandable Sections</strong>
    <p>Click the title of any Week to open its full content. Inside each week, click any Practice Block to reveal the detailed step-by-step instructions for that specific part of your session. Read each block fully before attempting it — then return to the page as a reference while you practise.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- EQUIPMENT -->
<section class="equip-section" id="equipment">
  <h2>Essential Equipment — What to Buy Before Week 1</h2>
  <div class="equip-grid">
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>The Violin (4/4 size)</strong>
      <p>For adults, you need a full-size 4/4 violin. Do not buy the cheapest possible instrument — a very poor instrument produces an unpleasant sound no matter your skill, which is demoralising. Spend £150–£400 / $180–$500 for a beginner outfit (violin + bow + case). Reliable brands: Eastman, Stentor, Fiddlershop. The top of the violin (the face) should be solid spruce — avoid "laminate top" instruments if possible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>The Bow</strong>
      <p>Your outfit will include a bow. Check that the bow hair is not frayed and the stick is not warped (sight down it like a rifle). A carbon fibre or Brazilwood bow is preferable to fibreglass for a beginner. Never touch the bow hair with your fingers — skin oils ruin it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Shoulder Rest</strong>
      <p>Non-negotiable. Without a shoulder rest, you will clamp the violin with your chin and jaw, causing tension and eventually pain. The Kun Bravo (£25/$30) or Bonmusica (£35/$45) are both excellent. It clips onto the lower bout of the violin and bridges the gap between the instrument and your shoulder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Rosin</strong>
      <p>The bow hair produces no sound on its own — it needs rosin (a cake of hardened tree resin) to grip the strings. Your outfit will likely include a cake. Apply it before every practice: draw the bow hair across the rosin surface 4–8 slow, full strokes. Do this until you can see a fine white dust on the hair. Too little = silent; too much = scratchy white powder on your instrument (wipe it off with a soft cloth after playing).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Clip-On Chromatic Tuner</strong>
      <p>A Snark SN-5 or D'Addario NS Micro (£10–£18/$12–$22) clips onto the scroll. It reads vibrations through the wood, so it works in a noisy room. You will use this every single day. Tune before practising — an out-of-tune violin trains your ear incorrectly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Metronome App</strong>
      <p>"Pro Metronome" (iOS/Android, free) is excellent. A metronome is a device that clicks at a steady pulse. You will use it constantly — it teaches your body to stay rhythmically consistent, which is one of the hardest things to learn on a stringed instrument.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Music Stand</strong>
      <p>You must not hunch over a table to read music — this destroys your posture. A basic folding music stand (£15/$18) places the music at eye level. Your head stays up, your back stays straight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="equip-item">
      <strong>Books to Order Now</strong>
      <p><em>Suzuki Violin School Vol. 1</em> (essential — this is your repertoire book). <em>Ševčík Op. 1 Part 1</em> (bow exercises — buy once you reach Week 4). These two books will sustain you through this entire course and beyond.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

